          dealing: 'Each player receives 3 cards face-down (hole cards). Four cards are placed face-down in the center as community cards.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Form the best five-card poker hand using any combination of your three hole cards and the four community cards.',
          gameplay: '1. **Ante**: All players post an ante. 2. **Initial Deal**: Each player receives 3 hole cards. 3. **First Betting Round**: Players bet based on their hole cards only. 4. **First Community Card**: Dealer reveals the first community card face-up. This is NOT automatically wild. 5. **Second Betting Round**: Players bet. 6. **Second Community Card**: Revealed. 7. **Third Betting Round**: Players bet. 8. **Third Community Card**: Revealed. 9. **Fourth Betting Round**: Players bet. 10. **Fourth Community Card**: Revealed. 11. **Final Betting Round**: Players bet. 12. **Showdown**: Remaining players reveal hands.',
          keyRules: [
            'The wild card is determined only after ALL four community cards are revealed.',
            'Until the final card, players must speculate on what the wild card might be.',
            'Standard poker hand rankings apply, with wild cards substituting for any rank/suit.',
            'If two community cards tie for lowest rank, both ranks are wild.',
            'Aces are high for determining the lowest wild card.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Start with strong hole cards that have flexibility. Three cards of different ranks give you more chances to pair up. Avoid playing hands where all three cards are the same suit (unless very high), as you only have one card to complete a flush.',
          intermediate: "Track potential wild cards. If your hole cards include a 2, 3, or 4, you have a good chance that your cards might become wild. Conversely, holding high cards (T, J, Q, K, A) means you're hoping the community cards stay low.",
          advanced: "The 'Lame Brain' Calculation: When the third community card is revealed, calculate the probability of the fourth card making your hole cards wild. If you hold a 5 and the community shows 8-7-3, there's a good chance your 5 becomes wild if the last card is 2, 3, 4, or 5. Use this to determine whether to bet aggressively or fold.",
          tips: [
            "Don't overvalue your hand early. The wild card can dramatically change hand values.",
            "If you have trips in your hole cards, you're in excellent position regardless of the wild card.",
            "Pay attention to opponents' betting patterns after each community card is revealed.",
            "The wild card often creates multiple full houses and quads at showdownâ€”don't be married to a strong hand early.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
